WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:17.100
AI TAKING OVER:&nbsp;

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:27.360
“AI will replace our jobs”. This is the most&nbsp;
ridiculous statement I’ve ever heard. Eversince&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:33.360
ChatGPT came out, there’s been this general fear&nbsp;
that AI will replace everyone’s jobs and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:39.720
all of our skills will suddenly become completely&nbsp;
useless. And it’s not surprising why people think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:45.240
this. For one, people have seen these tools in&nbsp;
action first hand. They’ve seen realistic photos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:51.300
spawning out of thin air, 3000 word essays being&nbsp;
typed out in 30 seconds, and hundreds of line code&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:57.660
being drafted up like it’s nothing. At the same&nbsp;
time, we also have all these articles that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:04.920
like AI will replace 47% of all US jobs within&nbsp;
the next 20 years or AI is set to replace 300&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:13.020
million jobs or AI could replace up to 8 in 10&nbsp;
jobs, expert says. And it’s not just journalists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:18.780
and media organizations who are making these bold&nbsp;
claims either. We have valedictorians from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:24.300
most elite colleges and CEOs from the biggest tech&nbsp;
companies that are all propagating this notion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:30.300
Elon, for example, has been claiming that we’ll&nbsp;
all need universal basic income because robots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:35.760
will take all our jobs. Meanwhile, Sundar Pichai&nbsp;
has been preaching that AI could be more profound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:43.320
than fire or electricity. But, you wanna know&nbsp;
a secret? Sundar has been saying the exact same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:49.920
thing for over 5 years now. That’s just his go to&nbsp;
statement when he’s pitching the importance and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:57.240
impact of Google’s AI. Also, remember that study&nbsp;
that was suggesting that 47% of all US jobs will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:03.060
be replaced by AI within the next 20 years?&nbsp;
Well, that study was published 10 years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:09.240
so theoretically, we should already be feeling the&nbsp;
pain. Yet, unemployment is still at near all time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:15.300
lows despite recession fears, high inflation, high&nbsp;
interest rates, and apparently AI taking over.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:22.260
And the reason is obvious, AI is not going&nbsp;
leave all of us jobless relying on some sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:28.800
of UBI from the government because AI will not&nbsp;
be replacing the value of humans, let me explain.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:35.700
HUMAN RESILIENCE:&nbsp;

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.220
To understand why AI doesn’t stand a chance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:43.860
all we have to do is look back at history. In&nbsp;
fact, we can rewind 12,000 years to the year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:50.160
10,000 BC. This probably feels like forever&nbsp;
ago but in the context of human development,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:55.740
it’s actually extremely recent. Modern humans are&nbsp;
thought to have come about sometime within the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.740 --> 00:03:03.480
past 200 to 300,000 years, so 10,000 BC is part&nbsp;
of the most recent 5% of human history. Up until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:08.940
this point, the life of the average human was&nbsp;
pretty simple. Virtually every single human spent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:14.340
the vast majority of their time on survival. They&nbsp;
would hunt for animals, gather berries and water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:19.980
create weapons using rocks and sticks, and ignite&nbsp;
fires to keep them warm and cook food. Basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:27.000
all of their time was just spent on survival. But,&nbsp;
somehow, around this time, some idiots around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:31.620
world figured out that if you put a seed into&nbsp;
the ground, pour some water on it, and give it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:39.060
some time, you could produce food on demand.&nbsp;
Now, imagine if these nomads were like “Brooo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:45.000
if we could just scale this agriculture thing,&nbsp;
man no one would have to work anymore especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:50.520
if we got good at it. Imagine if just 1% of the&nbsp;
population could make enough food for everyone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:58.020
Then we could all just sit around and never have&nbsp;
to worry about survival again.” Oh wait, that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:04.560
exactly how it is today. Most people in western&nbsp;
countries never have to worry about food but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:12.960
98.7% of Americans are not unemployed. In fact,&nbsp;
it’s the other way around. The people who produce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:18.300
all of our food are usually the least paid, the&nbsp;
least respected, and the least valued even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:23.580
they often work the longest, the hardest, and&nbsp;
are essential to our survival. Now, theoretically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:28.980
speaking we could’ve made that nomad dream a&nbsp;
reality. We could’ve had some sort of system where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:34.680
all of us only have to work like 1 week every&nbsp;
year on the fields. The rest of the time, we could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:41.520
just sit in our huts and have food handed to us.&nbsp;
Thinking back, this did start to happen. As the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:48.600
efficiency of agriculture skyrocketed the amount&nbsp;
of free time people had also skyrocketed. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:54.960
people didn’t just opt to sit around; instead,&nbsp;
they started mastering crafts. They became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.960 --> 00:05:00.480
skilled bakers, carpenters, butchers, soldiers,&nbsp;
stonemasons, and tailors. We spent our time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:05.760
building entire cities with hundreds of roles,&nbsp;
jobs, and mechanics all centered around these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:12.240
agricultural hubs. And agriculture is just one&nbsp;
example. Such shifts have occurred thousands of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:17.340
times during human history during the advents of&nbsp;
concrete, electricity, light bulbs, telephones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:22.140
locomotives, cars, planes, trucks, highways,&nbsp;
vaccines, computers, smartphones, the internet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:30.660
and who knows what else. I mean just think&nbsp;
about this. 60% of all workers are currently in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:37.140
occupations that did not even exist in 1940. Let&nbsp;
me phrase that in a different way. The majority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:45.540
of all working people are working at jobs that&nbsp;
did not even exist a single lifetime ago. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:50.640
how in the world is the advent of AI any&nbsp;
different from what we’ve been doing over the past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:56.940
12,000 years? Well, many would argue that it’s the&nbsp;
speed of AI progress that makes it so dangerous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:04.320
Not only will AI replace low level factory jobs or&nbsp;
cashier jobs but it’ll also replace white collar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:12.540
jobs like doctors, lawyers, and engineers. And&nbsp;
to this, I once again call BS. Let me explain.

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:20.040
THIS TIME IS NOT DIFFERENT:&nbsp;

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:26.100
People often like to claim that it’s the speed&nbsp;
of AI advancement that makes it so disruptive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:34.380
but this too is easily disprovable. Yes, AI is&nbsp;
advancing faster than anything we’ve seen before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:43.200
but guess what so did every other revolutionary&nbsp;
technology. That’s kind of why it’s revolutionary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:48.960
It took agriculture 10,000 years to catch&nbsp;
on but it only took a thousand years for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:53.700
cities to catch on. It only took hundreds of&nbsp;
years for electricity, only decades for tech,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.700 --> 00:07:00.180
and only took years for smartphones. So, it’s only&nbsp;
natural that AI is able to catch on within months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:07.320
but really it’s not even that fast. Researchers&nbsp;
predict that AI’s computational power will double&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:15.360
every 6 to 10 months. But this isn’t even all that&nbsp;
crazy when you consider Moore’s law. Back in 1965,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:20.880
Gordon Moore, the eventual founder of Intel,&nbsp;
predicted that the number of transistors on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:27.420
integrated circuits will double every single year&nbsp;
and he was spot on. Over the next 10 years, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:33.900
number of transistors on microchips jumped from a&nbsp;
few hundred to tens of thousands. But after this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:40.260
fast initial jump, Gordon would actually revise&nbsp;
his prediction as progress slowed to transistors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:47.040
doubling every 2 years which has largely held true&nbsp;
over the past 50 years. So, objectively speaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:52.860
the intelligence of tech was advancing the&nbsp;
fastest back in the late 60s and early 70s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:58.680
But if you extrapolated that to argue that&nbsp;
this was the fastest period of tech adoption,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:04.860
you would’ve been completely wrong. Microsoft&nbsp;
and Apple weren’t even a thing yet. And there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:10.140
was still decades till Google and Facebook&nbsp;
would even be founded. Really, it wasn’t till&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:16.980
the 2000s and especially after the launch of the&nbsp;
iPhone that tech adoption became ubiquitous. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:23.580
this isn’t to say that it’ll take decades for AI&nbsp;
to become ubiquitous. It’s obviously advancing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:28.860
and being adopted faster than computers but&nbsp;
it’s still naturally going to take time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:34.320
The bottomline is that with each and every&nbsp;
one of these revolutionary introductions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:40.920
it’s humans who are creating these changes and&nbsp;
propagating them. So, naturally, these changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:46.320
will be limited by the laws of supply and demand.&nbsp;
Just because you make a great AI doesn’t mean that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:53.040
it’ll be instantly adopted. It’ll only be adopted&nbsp;
when there is a demand for it and there will only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:58.020
be an demand for it when the technology and&nbsp;
a large portion of society are ready for it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:07.680
So, this time is not different. In fact, I’d&nbsp;
wager that AI will actually catch on much slower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:13.380
than you might expect and the best example of&nbsp;
this is the failed automation of factories.

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:21.000
THE AUTOMATION THAT NEVER HAPPENED:&nbsp;

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:26.580
Before everyone was worried about AI taking&nbsp;
over white collar jobs, people were scared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:32.940
about automation taking over factory jobs.&nbsp;
And this makes sense given that back in 1979,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:40.560
factory employment was at an all time high. 19.6&nbsp;
million Americans worked factory jobs, so fancy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:47.520
robot arms and assembly lines obviously made&nbsp;
them feel uneasy. And they were right, factory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:56.520
jobs did indeed decline by 35%....over the next 40&nbsp;
years. In other words, all of these crazy computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.520 --> 00:10:02.040
chips that went from a thousand transistors&nbsp;
to 50 billion transistors weren’t even able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:09.480
to eliminate half of factory jobs over the next 40&nbsp;
years. They weren’t even eliminating 1% per year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:14.760
And that’s not even accounting for all of the&nbsp;
factory jobs that were shipped overseas as opposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:21.900
to being replaced by robots. The number one&nbsp;
reason for this is that robots suck at handling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:28.260
edge cases. Our AI overlord Elon, is actually the&nbsp;
most familiar with this as he was nearly driven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:34.380
into bankruptcy because of it. You see, as a&nbsp;
staunch AI and automation supporter, I don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:39.660
think you’d be surprised to hear that Elon tried&nbsp;
to create a completely automated Tesla factory.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:46.380
He hired the best software and AI engineers on the&nbsp;
planet, sunk billions of dollars into building his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:51.780
grand gigafactory and had hundreds of thousands&nbsp;
of people waiting to receive their Model 3s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:58.860
Theoretically, all he had to do was press the go&nbsp;
button on the factory, deliver a bunch of cars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:05.400
and make a crap ton of money. Easy right?&nbsp;
Well, all of this changed when he actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:10.920
pressed the go button. There’s really no&nbsp;
pleasant way to put this. The reality was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:17.580
that his automated factory was absolute trash.&nbsp;
One small misalignment in in the sheets of metal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:22.440
and the robots couldn’t screw in the bolt.&nbsp;
One small misalignement in the paintshop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:29.160
and the robot would paint the window. Such matters&nbsp;
are trivial for humans to handle but for robots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:34.980
such matters made them completely ineffective.&nbsp;
Elon found himself constantly stopping the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:39.660
production line to fix all the errors that&nbsp;
these robots were making. This destroyed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:44.220
productivity of his factories, leading to Tesla&nbsp;
burning billions of dollars and getting single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.220 --> 00:11:49.560
digit weeks away from bankruptcy. You know how&nbsp;
Tesla recovered from this manufacturing hell?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:56.100
Well, everyone including the tech guys had to work&nbsp;
on the factory line and work 100 plus hours every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.100 --> 00:12:02.760
week just to get Tesla to stop burning cash.&nbsp;
Aka, Tesla had to deautomate their factory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:10.020
and Elon would end up admitting “Yes, excessive&nbsp;
automation at Tesla was a mistake. To be precise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:17.400
my mistake. Humans are underrated.” If this is the&nbsp;
case with simple repetitive factory automation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:23.880
how in the world is it realistic to assume that&nbsp;
AI can just automate software engineering just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:30.720
because it can write algorithms or solve hard&nbsp;
leetcode problems. It really makes no sense at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:37.080
all. The vast majority of a software engineer's&nbsp;
time is not spent on doing the fun obvious stuff.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:43.920
In fact, the obvious stuff takes no time at all.&nbsp;
The vast majority of time is spent on doing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:49.620
boring but essential stuff. How do we handle all&nbsp;
of these different errors? How do we scale this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:55.560
code seamlessly? How do we address all the edge&nbsp;
cases? This is precisely where engineers spend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.560 --> 00:13:03.180
their time and this is precisely what AI cannot&nbsp;
do. I mean, just look at Tesla autopilot. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:08.460
were able to get lane assist on highways working&nbsp;
extremely fast, but getting it to work everywhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.460 --> 00:13:15.120
else is proving extremely difficult. Now, with&nbsp;
that being said, it would also be stupid to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:21.900
that AI won’t affect software engineering. It&nbsp;
absolutely will but we’re talking about a slow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.900 --> 00:13:26.880
shift away like with manufacturing as opposed&nbsp;
to something that just transpires overnight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:33.540
just because ChatGPT has a 100% accuracy on&nbsp;
Leetcode. And this brings us into the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:39.720
argument regarding AI replacing jobs which is&nbsp;
that it’ll replace more jobs than it creates.

00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:49.620
THE JOB DEFICIT DEBUNKED:
The idea that AI will take away more jobs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:57.720
than it creates is really quite stupid because AI&nbsp;
nor any new technology is not a zero sum game and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:02.760
it’s quite limiting to look at it from that&nbsp;
perspective. Take the internet for example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.760 --> 00:14:08.820
In the early days, the internet was largely used&nbsp;
for research purposes. What if people thought that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.820 --> 00:14:13.380
the internet would take away jobs just because&nbsp;
it would put a lot of libraries out of business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.380 --> 00:14:21.300
and in return only give a 1000 jobs to coders?&nbsp;
Obviously, that statement is laughable today but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:26.580
is it really that far fetched back in the day?&nbsp;
40 years ago, no one would’ve even been able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.580 --> 00:14:31.800
fathom the idea of multi hundred billion dollar&nbsp;
social media companies, e commerce platforms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:36.780
cloud providers, database solutions, streaming&nbsp;
giants, fintech offerings, food delivery, ride&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.780 --> 00:14:43.800
sharing, online booking services, the list really&nbsp;
goes on forever. It’ll be the same way with AI.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:49.920
From our current position, we can only envision&nbsp;
how AI will disrupt the jobs and careers that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:55.500
we already know. We have no chance of even&nbsp;
conceptuatlizing all of the jobs and careers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.500 --> 00:15:02.520
that AI will create. 15 years ago, it wasn’t even&nbsp;
possible to make money as an “influencer”. Today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.520 --> 00:15:09.540
being a YouTuber is the number 1 dream job in&nbsp;
America. Also, not only did tech create a bunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:15.810
of jobs but it created the new first class. Think&nbsp;
about all of the richest people in the world. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.810 --> 00:15:21.000
are owners of tech companies, executives of&nbsp;
tech companies, employees of tech companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:24.960
partners of tech companies, affiliates of&nbsp;
tech companies, investors of tech companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.960 --> 00:15:29.760
and so on. Truth be told, if you weren’t&nbsp;
involved in tech within the past 20 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:36.540
you were probably left behind. And it’s no wonder&nbsp;
why. These companies are able to create value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:43.680
faster and at larger scales than ever before.&nbsp;
This will only be truer with AI as AI makes work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:50.940
magnitudes more efficient. So, not only will AI&nbsp;
not make everyone jobless hobos but it’ll actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.940 --> 00:15:57.420
create an unprecedented number of insanely highly&nbsp;
paying jobs along with hundreds of trillionaires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.420 --> 00:16:03.480
because the only way that it plays out any&nbsp;
other way is if humans fundamentally change.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:10.740
If humans fundamentally change and somehow&nbsp;
become satisfied with what we already have.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:16.620
The current tech we have, the current economy&nbsp;
we have, and the current society that we have.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.220 --> 00:16:23.940
As long as people are always striving for more&nbsp;
and more, you can rest assured that we’ll never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:30.600
end up in some sort of AI dystopia where robots&nbsp;
do everything and humans do nothing. There will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:37.140
always be something for us to do and if there&nbsp;
isn’t, you can be sure as hell that we’ll make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.140 --> 00:16:44.400
something to do and that’s why the idea of AI&nbsp;
replacing everyone’s jobs is simply ridiculous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:50.880
AI content, however, is ruining YouTube. If you&nbsp;
wanna learn more about that, check out this video.

